Nanoscale thermal transport II — Cahill and colleagues (@th-nano-review: 0, is #reference, #part-of @th-sources) { r_30EZYRYJ20TDTWN3JXWD9KWSFG
David G. Cahill and colleagues. Nanoscale thermal transport. II. 2003–2012. Applied Physics Reviews 1, 011305 (2014), DOI 10.1063/1.4832615. [MIT institutional author record](https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/eb36cc3f-2f43-4c54-929c-4034bfb05172) · [Coauthor-hosted full article](https://web.stanford.edu/group/fan/publication/Cahill_AppliedPhysicsReview_1_011305_2014.pdf).

Read the complete review and the full primary methods behind a chosen interface, thin-film or carrier-transport question. Relate experimental observables to transport length/time scales and competing microscopic explanations. Follow later work and corrections before making a current research claim.

The title's 2003–2012 is the reviewed period, not the publication date. The review is a substantial bridge from continuum heat transfer into materials and device physics, while the full statistical, quantum and semiconductor subjects retain their separate scope. Inspect the actual author/article rights; all articles, figures and datasets remain external.

